   "why": "The Census answers the second and not the first. B11005 puts 1,477 of 4,529 households \u2014 32.6% \u2014 with a child under 18, which is what people mean when they say \"about 30% of taxpaying homes\", and on that reading the claim is fair. But that count includes children at Monty Tech, at private and charter schools, and children not yet of school age, so it is a CEILING rather than the figure. The arithmetic suggests the gap is real: 1,477 households against roughly 1,745 school-age children is about 1.2 per household, low enough that a meaningful share of those homes have only pre-school children. Nothing published joins a child to a household to a school. \u2014 closes: a count of DISTINCT HOUSEHOLDS with at least one child enrolled, which the district can produce from its own student information system and DESE cannot, because DESE counts children and the Census counts households and neither knows about the other."
  },
  {
   "side": "people",
   "what": "Who actually lives in Lunenburg \u2014 how many are over 65, how many households have a child in the schools, and what each group can afford?",
   "why": "ANSWERED IN PART, 11 September 2026, and the part that remains is the part the argument actually runs on. The Census tables named below were fetched and are loaded as `census_acs`: 11,804 \u00b1 19 residents, 1,979 \u00b1 309 of them 65 or over, 4,529 \u00b1 235 households, 1,477 \u00b1 198 with a child under 18, median household income $109,753 \u00b1 $12,275 and $72,935 \u00b1 $8,677 where the householder is 65 or over. See /lunenburg-by-the-numbers, where every figure carries its margin. WHAT SURVIVES is the third clause \u2014 what each group can AFFORD. Income is not wealth, not savings, not equity and not a tax bill, and nothing published joins a property tax payment to the age of the person who paid it; the two rows below carry the two halves of what is still missing. \u2014 closes: the town census, which counts every resident by name, age and household rather than sampling them, held by the Town Clerk."
  },
  {
   "side": "people",
   "what": "How much of the property tax levy is paid by households headed by someone 65 or over?",
   "why": "The Census now gives income by age of householder \u2014 a household headed by someone 65 or over has a median income of $72,935 \u00b1 $8,677, against $129,183 \u00b1 $21,150 for a 45-to-64 household \u2014 and that is income, not tax. The override argument is about a BILL: what a $3,000,000 override adds to a household is quoted at meetings as a town-wide average, and the town publishes no split of the levy by who pays it, by age or by anything else. Nor does it publish how many households take the statutory elderly exemptions and abatements that would change the answer. So \"seniors on fixed incomes cannot carry it\" cannot be checked in either direction, and neither can its opposite. \u2014 closes: the Board of Assessors\u2019 commitment list with the Clause 41A, 41C and 17D exemptions granted, joined to the town census by age of owner \u2014 both held by the Town and neither published."
  }
